# Lane Router

Route a described task to the coding harness, model and fleet machine that fit it best, and spend the AI subscription pools that are closest to expiring unused. The input is a plain task description. The output is a lane plan with one lane or several parallel lanes, each named and ready to propose to the user.

## Gather the facts

- Read the pool state first from the hourly pool cache, via the pool_status skill: run `python3 ~/.agents/skills/pool_status/scripts/pool_status.py` and read its burn order before choosing anything. If the cache is missing or flagged STALE (older than 2 hours), fall back to the live census helper on the coordinator VM: `~/workspace/.venv-ssh/bin/python ~/.agents/skills/lane_router/scripts/pools.py`.

- Read the burn order the helper prints, which ranks pools by hours to reset and remaining capacity, and treat pools whose quota is unverified or in error as unknown rather than empty.

- Read the routing matrix in `~/workspace/projects/fleet-ops/files/subscription-work-division-2026-10-17.md` for the signed-off task type to pool mapping, and check `~/workspace/capabilities-index.yaml` for which machines offer the harness you want and which subscription each bundle draws on.

- The helper, the census and the master index live on the coordinator VM (Bossman), and the helper runs there. On a fleet machine, work from the local read-only index snapshot when one is present; when it is not, the coordinator supplies the census output — the plan travels, not the data. The method below is the same either way.

- Ask the user one question when the task description leaves a constraint open that would change the routing, such as a deadline, a target repo or a machine the work must stay on.

## Apply the hard constraints first

- Filter to machines that satisfy the task's machine bounds before any pool preference, because an SDK, a dataset or a protected tree on one machine beats every quota argument.

- Keep work off a machine the user has restricted for that work, and respect protected trees and per-machine bans recorded in memory and in `~/AGENTS.md`.

- Never route a review lane to the pool that authored the change under review, and route hostile or adversarial review to a different pool than the author used.

- Keep the pool the user is actively coding on reserved, and route proposed lanes around it.

- Leave a pool out of the plan when its machines show a lapsed login the user has not fixed, and say so in the plan rather than silently substituting.

## Score fit, then expiry

- Classify the task against the routing matrix and name its type in the plan, such as frontier feature work, PR fix cycle, verdict review, hostile review, test writing, audit sweep, bulk transform, design-sensitive frontend, learning project or long autonomous lane.

- Prefer the harness with the strongest fit for that task type, using the matrix primary first and its overflow order when the primary pool is capped, lapsed or a poor burn choice.

- Break ties and choose among overflow options by burn urgency, taking the pool with the least time to reset and the most remaining capacity first, because quota that expires unused is the waste this router exists to prevent.

- Weigh a pool's window size against the lane's appetite, so a multi-hour autonomous lane lands on a weekly pool and a short sharp lane can drain a five-hour window that is about to roll.

- Treat a pool already at its cap as unavailable until its reset, and note the reset time in the plan when a lane is worth deferring to it.

## Shape the plan

- Split the task into parallel lanes whenever it decomposes into independent slices, such as disjoint file areas, separate repos, an author lane plus an independent reviewer lane, or a build lane plus a test lane, and give each lane a disjoint scope in writing.

- Keep slices on different pools when the split allows it, so no single cap stalls the whole task and the burn spreads across pools nearing expiry.

- Name each lane with the fleet session convention of project then lane, and state its task checkout location from the capabilities index when the index gives one.

- Give every lane the harness's most powerful model at its highest effort setting, and verify the current top model name at launch time because model names drift.

- Size the plan honestly, so a task one lane can finish well does not become a swarm for show, and say when a single lane is the right answer.

## Present the plan

- Present each proposed lane as one line covering lane name, harness and model with effort, machine, subscription pool and the one-line reason it fits and burns well.

- Add the burn note after the lanes, naming the pools the plan spends, their remaining capacity and reset times, and any pool left stranded that the plan could not use.

- Name the alternatives you rejected in one line each when a reasonable reader would ask, such as the frontier pool saved for harder work or a machine skipped over a lapsed login.

- Stop at the proposal, because launching a new coding lane needs the user's explicit confirmation for that lane, and hand over ready-to-launch briefs when you have them.

- Refresh the census and rerun the helper when the user returns to a plan later, because a plan built on stale quota is a wrong plan.

